    body: "Nigeria sends more medical tourists to India than almost any other African country. Kidney transplant is one of the top reasons — and for good reason.\n\nKidney Transplant Cost in India for Nigerian Patients\n\nKidney transplant in India costs $8,400 to $18,000 (approximately NGN 13,000,000 to NGN 28,000,000 at current rates). This compares to $50,000 to $150,000 or more for the same procedure at private hospitals in South Africa, UK, or USA. Even after flights and accommodation, Nigerian patients save 70 to 80% by choosing India.\n\nThe cost includes surgery, hospital stay of 4 to 6 weeks, ICU, medications during hospitalisation, and basic post-operative care. Accommodation for an accompanying family member and local transport is additional.\n\nBest Hospitals in India for Kidney Transplant — Trusted by Nigerian Patients\n\nApollo Hospitals Delhi and Chennai: One of the largest kidney transplant programmes in Asia. Dedicated international patient services with coordinators who have worked with Nigerian patients for over 15 years. Medanta The Medicity Gurugram: Over 600 organ transplants annually. State-of-the-art ICU and nephrology support. Fortis Memorial Research Institute: Experienced transplant team with strong outcomes data. Max Super Speciality Hospital Delhi: Strong kidney transplant programme with dedicated African patient support.\n\nAll hospitals are NABH-accredited and have handled hundreds of Nigerian patients.\n\nWhy Nigerian Patients Choose India for Kidney Transplant\n\nCost: 75 to 85% lower than South Africa, UK, or USA. Waiting times: No waiting list — surgery scheduled within 2 to 4 weeks of arrival. Quality: JCI-accredited hospitals with outcomes matching global standards. English: All hospitals communicate in English. Halal food available on request. Direct flights: Lagos to Delhi (8 to 9 hours) via multiple airlines.\n\nLiving Donor vs Deceased Donor Transplant\n\nIndia's Transplant of Human Organs Act permits living related donors (spouse, parents, children, siblings). Most Nigerian patients travel with a related donor. Deceased donor transplants from the government waitlist are available but have longer waiting times. MySurgery coordinates the full medical evaluation of both recipient and donor simultaneously to minimise your time in India.\n\nMedical Visa for Nigerian Patients\n\nStep 1: MySurgery provides a hospital invitation letter within 48 hours of your decision. Step 2: Apply for Indian Medical Visa (MV) at the Indian High Commission in Lagos (Abuja application centre also available). Step 3: Visa is typically granted in 5 to 7 business days. You can bring 2 family members on Medical Attendant Visa (MX). Step 4: MySurgery provides a comprehensive support letter for the visa application.\n\nWhat Happens on Arrival in India\n\nMySurgery team picks you up from the airport and takes you to your pre-arranged accommodation near the hospital. Pre-transplant evaluation (HLA matching, cross-match, medical fitness) is completed within 3 to 5 days. If compatible, transplant is scheduled. Hospital stay is 4 to 6 weeks. MySurgery coordinators are present throughout for all hospital communication and documentation.\n\nWhatsApp MySurgery on +91 8588967050 or visit mysurgery.in/contact for a free treatment plan within 48 hours.",
